388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 53 0 R
/T (Q2. Why do LRU and Optimal never show this? They are stack algorithms. For any)
/E (Q2. Why do LRU and Optimal never show this? They are stack algorithms. For any)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
389 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 53 0 R
/T (reference string, the set of pages in memory with n frames is always a subset of the set in memory with n+1 frames \(the inclusion or stack property\). So an extra frame can only keep more useful pages and can never cause an extra fault. FIFO chooses victims by arrival order rather than usefulness, so it has no such property.)
/E (reference string, the set of pages in memory with n frames is always a subset of the set in memory with n+1 frames \(the inclusion or stack property\). So an extra frame can only keep more useful pages and can never cause an extra fault. FIFO chooses victims by arrival order rather than usefulness, so it has no such property.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
390 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 53 0 R
/T (LRU better. At step 12, page 1 faults and all four frames are full. FIFO evicts page 4 because it is the oldest, yet page 4 is needed again at steps 13 and 15, so FIFO faults again on 4 and later on 3. LRU evicts page 5, which was last used at step 7 and is never needed again, so the later references to 2, 4 and 3 are all hits. Result: 8 faults for FIFO against 6 for LRU. FIFO equal. This string has only 5 distinct pages, so with 4 frames just one eviction is ever needed \(step 17, when page 4 first appears\). FIFO and LRU both evict page 5, so all three algorithms give the minimum of 5 faults. This case is a tie rather than a FIFO win, which the assignment allows \("better than or equal to"\). Overall, LRU beats FIFO when recently used pages are reused soon, and results depend on the access pattern.)
/E (LRU better. At step 12, page 1 faults and all four frames are full. FIFO evicts page 4 because it is the oldest, yet page 4 is needed again at steps 13 and 15, so FIFO faults again on 4 and later on 3. LRU evicts page 5, which was last used at step 7 and is never needed again, so the later references to 2, 4 and 3 are all hits. Result: 8 faults for FIFO against 6 for LRU. FIFO equal. This string has only 5 distinct pages, so with 4 frames just one eviction is ever needed \(step 17, when page 4 first appears\). FIFO and LRU both evict page 5, so all three algorithms give the minimum of 5 faults. This case is a tie rather than a FIFO win, which the assignment allows \("better than or equal to"\). Overall, LRU beats FIFO when recently used pages are reused soon, and results depend on the access pattern.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
