527 0 obj
<<
/Title (Optimal looks ahead in the reference string and evicts the page whose next use is farthest away, or which is never used again. It gives the minimum possible number of faults, but it cannot be built in a real OS because future references are unknown. It is used only as a benchmark. Tie rule used in this project: if several pages are never used again, the one that appears first in the frames is replaced.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 470 0 R
>>
endobj
528 0 obj
<<
/Title (The program is a single Jupyter notebook that uses pandas to display the comparison table. All frames start empty and fill in slot order. A victim is replaced in the same slot, so frame contents in each trace row are shown in stable slot order.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 471 0 R
/Next 590 0 R
>>
endobj
529 0 obj
<<
/Title (A single function, simulate\(\), implements all three policies; only the victim-selection rule differs. FIFO takes the front of a queue of arrival order, LRU takes the resident page with the smallest last-used index, and Optimal takes the resident page whose next use is farthest away \(ties go to the first page in the frames\). Each trace row is a dictionary with the keys step, page, frames, result \(H or F\) and replaced \(the evicted page, or -\). Hit ratio is hits / n and fault ratio is faults / n, where n is the length of the reference string.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 471 0 R
/Prev 591 0 R
>>
endobj
